  "query_preview": "..We've had several periods of showers and\nthunderstorms moving across the forecast area today associated\nwith a upper level trof that was along the Colorado river this\nafternoon. Rainfall amounts so far today, as of 2 pm, have \nranged mostly less than 0.40\" with isolated totals up to 0.75\".\nLatest CAMs keep activity going late into the evening and if \nwe see some breaks in the clouds to get some additional \ndestabilization before sunset, a few storms could briefly get \nfrisky with severe wind/hail and brief heavy rain and localized \nflooding concerns. \n\nSaturday: Moist southwest flow aloft continues and although the\narea will be in between systems, isolated showers will be \naround producing light rainfall amounts. \n\nLate Saturday night into Sunday night: A strong southern steam \nsystem, riding the subtropical jet, will be approaching the area\nlate Saturday night as the west coast upper level trof moves \ninland. A strong atmospheric river (AR), level 3 on a AR 1-5 \nscale, will bring near record to record levels of PWAT of (1.25\"\nto 1.50\") for November. As this AR moves over the area, expect\nwidespread moderate to localizes heavy rain and isolated embedded \nthunderstorms on Sunday. Upslope flow into the mountains will\nenhance rainfall rates/totals. Most of the area is under slight\nrisk from WPC for excessive rainfall with a very small snippet \nof far northern Graham and Greenlee counties under moderate \nrisk. We are looking at rainfall records for November 1st \nfalling across much of the area. A Flood Watch is in effect for \nall of SE AZ from 11 pm Saturday to 5 am Monday. What is \nuncertain is how much rainfall Sunday into early Monday. Below \nis a look at the expected totals, high and low end totals.\n\nExpected: \nValleys 0.50\"-1.50\", Mountains 0.50\"-2+\" \nLow end (9 in 10 chance): \nValleys 0.10\"-0.90\", Mountains 0.30\"-1.5\"\nHigh end (1 in 10 chance): \nValleys 1.75\"-2.75\", Mountains 2\"-4\" locally higher\n\nWith the expected rain on Sunday into early Monday we will see \na response in the area rivers, creeks and washes across SE AZ. \nSee hydrology section below for further details.\n\nThereafter, we'll get into a quieter weather regime early next \nweek. Any lingering precipitation should come to an end Monday \nmorning with shortwave ridging and dry conditions Tuesday and \nWednesday. Thursday still looks dry as well, then more \nuncertainty thereafter as another system potentially impacts our\narea next Friday and beyond with more precipitation chances. \n\nDaytimes temperatures will mostly be below normal through the \nforecast period, with the coolest highs Sunday and Monday.",
